# Shell scripts must check out with LF, whatever the platform.
#
# This repository's shell scripts are run through WSL, and several of them are
# executed directly rather than passed to an interpreter. On Windows with
# `core.autocrlf=true` - the default that Git for Windows offers, and what is
# set on the development machine this was found on - checkout rewrites them
# with CRLF. The kernel then reads the shebang as `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, treats
# the carriage return as part of the interpreter name, and reports:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# Which is an accurate message about a file nobody edited. The failure appears
# only after a checkout that rewrites the file, so a script can work for weeks
# and then stop at a `git reset --hard` with no change to its contents - which
# is how it was found.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf for these paths on every platform, so the working
# copy matches what the interpreter needs rather than what the OS prefers.
*.sh text eol=lf
